"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:05,783\nA map of the learning brain\n\n3\n00:00:08,783 --> 00:00:11,027\nWhere in the brain is a memory kept?\n\n4\n00:00:11,373 --> 00:00:12,905\nIt is a natural question,\n\n5\n00:00:12,956 --> 00:00:15,194\nand the answer surprises most people.\n\n6\n00:00:15,547 --> 00:00:17,633\nThere is no single memory center.\n\n7\n00:00:17,949 --> 00:00:20,498\nLearning is spread across the whole brain.\n\n8\n00:00:22,168 --> 00:00:23,608\nStart with the outer layer,\n\n9\n00:00:23,628 --> 00:00:24,371\nthe cortex.\n\n10\n00:00:24,664 --> 00:00:27,053\nIt folds into four lobes on each side.\n\n11\n00:00:27,508 --> 00:00:29,763\nThe frontal lobe, behind your forehead,\n\n12\n00:00:29,783 --> 00:00:31,763\nplans and holds ideas in mind.\n\n13\n00:00:32,297 --> 00:00:34,093\nThe parietal lobe, at the top,\n\n14\n00:00:34,110 --> 00:00:36,375\ntracks space, attention and number.\n\n15\n00:00:36,904 --> 00:00:38,729\nThe temporal lobe, by your ear,\n\n16\n00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:41,907\nhandles sound, language and the meaning of things.\n\n17\n00:00:42,182 --> 00:00:44,132\nAnd the occipital lobe,\n\n18\n00:00:44,152 --> 00:00:45,838\nat the back, is where seeing begins.\n\n19\n00:00:46,008 --> 00:00:48,349\nTucked inside are older structures.\n\n20\n00:00:48,702 --> 00:00:52,268\nThe hippocampus helps form new memories of facts and events.\n\n21\n00:00:52,648 --> 00:00:55,806\nBeside it, the amygdala marks what feels important.\n\n22\n00:00:56,389 --> 00:00:59,678\nDeeper still, the basal ganglia build habits,\n\n23\n00:00:59,806 --> 00:01:02,414\nand the cerebellum, below the back of the brain,\n\n24\n00:01:02,513 --> 00:01:04,778\nfine tunes movement and timing.\n\n25\n00:01:04,948 --> 00:01:08,605\nAll of this runs on about eighty six billion neurons.\n\n26\n00:01:08,951 --> 00:01:11,453\nLearning changes the connections between them.\n\n27\n00:01:11,905 --> 00:01:14,479\nLinks that are used together grow stronger.\n\n28\n00:01:16,149 --> 00:01:17,154\nSo a single memory,\n\n29\n00:01:17,174 --> 00:01:19,278\nlike your first day at a new school,\n\n30\n00:01:19,486 --> 00:01:21,355\nis not stored in one spot.\n\n31\n00:01:21,898 --> 00:01:26,608\nIts sights, sounds, feelings and meaning are held in different regions,\n\n32\n00:01:26,853 --> 00:01:29,192\nand recalling it brings them back together.\n\n33\n00:01:30,362 --> 00:01:33,402\nPatients with damage to one region show the pattern.\n\n34\n00:01:33,887 --> 00:01:37,619\nSomeone can lose the ability to form new memories of events,\n\n35\n00:01:37,867 --> 00:01:39,570\nyet still learn a new skill.\n\n36\n00:01:40,090 --> 00:01:43,347\nDifferent kinds of learning lean on different parts of the brain.\n\n37\n00:01:43,517 --> 00:01:46,377\nEach video in this collection visits one region: what it does,\n\n38\n00:01:46,772 --> 00:01:50,190\nhow we know, and what it means for learning.\n\n39\n00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:53,044\nWhy it matters for learning.\n\n40\n00:01:53,532 --> 00:01:56,624\nThe strongest learning engages many regions at once: seeing,\n\n41\n00:01:56,883 --> 00:02:00,200\nhearing, doing, feeling and explaining.\n\n42\n00:02:01,570 --> 00:02:03,008\nOne brain, many parts,\n\n43\n00:02:03,028 --> 00:02:03,811\none team."